  prose(<<~'CANONICAL_CHRONICLE', origin: :published)
    The tunnel had no name yet. That was the thrill of it—every step cut new ice, every breath fogged against stone no penguin had touched since the deep froze shut. Augur ☽'Silvsiaelrous~ ran her flipper along the wall before she spoke, as she always did, letting the mineral grain answer first.
    
    "Volcanic glass," she said. "Layered. Old."
    
    Her companion, ☽'profundius, a tunnel-runner ten seasons her junior, held the fire-core lantern higher. The vein they'd followed down from the mid-galleries had branched, narrowed, and opened into a pocket chamber no wider than a nesting alcove. The air tasted of deep-earth and sulfur. On a shelf of natural basalt, resting as if placed there by careful flippers, sat a tome.
    
    She touched it. Warm. Warmer than the lantern, warmer than the fire-core amulet at her throat. The binding was bone—femur-carved clasps, shadow-steel spine—and the volcanic glass cover threw back no light at all, as if it had been drinking the dark for centuries.
    
    "☽'profundius. Kill the lantern."
    
    He looked at her. She didn't look back. She was already opening the cover, and he'd worked with her long enough to know that when Silvsiaelrous stopped blinking, something was reading her as much as she was reading it.
    
    The dark came down. Total. The kind of dark that has texture—you feel the walls close, feel the ice's weight above, feel the Berg's volcanic heart ticking far below like something alive. And in the dark, the pages bloomed. Bioluminescent text, fungal-pale, rising from compressed ash like frost forming on still water. Script she shouldn't have been able to read. She read it anyway. Her frost-scars burned where the Glacial Crystalline Spiral language was carved into her own flesh, and the ice read her back, and the tome was warm, warm, warm against her flippers like a creature that had been waiting.
    
    She read for three hours. ☽'profundius sat in the dark and listened to her breathing change—steady, then shallow, then so slow he pressed his flipper to the wall to feel for tremors, afraid the chamber was losing air.
    
    When the lantern came back, Silvsiaelrous was holding the book against her chest. Her fire-core amulet had dimmed to a red thread. Her eyes were wet, though she'd deny it later.
    
    "It's a record," she said. "Of everything under the ice. Everything that was here before us."
    
    She carried it out of the unmapped tunnel and into Nightfall Shelf, where the Order's archivists took one look at the volcanic glass and the bone clasps and the text that burned away under aurora-light, and gave it a name that would outlast every penguin who touched it. ☽'somunis' Mist~Record. The tome of what the mist remembers.
    
    Silvsiaelrous was its first complete reader. The spine bore no notches then.
    
    ---
    
    Three generations later, the spine bore one.
    
    Noctisum~ held the Mist~Record the way a guild-master holds a debt—carefully, aware of its weight, aware that others were watching him be aware. The volcanic glass had been oiled by Order archivists until it gleamed like wet obsidian. The bone clasps were tight; the femur-carved hinges moved without sound. Someone had wrapped the lower binding in bioluminescent cloth, and the faint glow gave the tome the look of something sacred, which was the point. Everything in ☽'scar Ramtatus~ that season was performance.
    
    The single notch in the spine was for Silvsiaelrous. Missing; confirmed lost; a name the Fire Keepers now recited into the deep ice with the other dead miners, though she hadn't been a miner, and she wasn't confirmed anything. The ice had taken her during the Abyss Collapse Tide or she'd walked into it. The distinction mattered less each year.
    
    Noctisum~ set the Mist~Record on obsidian ice beside Dagger☽ Vaesacsorius~ and addressed the gathered factions. The Wake-Singers on one side; the Icebound Exchange on the other; the ☽'shadow Covens' Order filling every shadow in between, their fire-keepers stationed at the chamber's four vents like priests who'd learned to stand like soldiers.
    
    "Every act," he said, "spoken aloud. Every sacrifice named. Every theft, every mercy. Within one lunar cycle, or the actor becomes unrecorded."
    
    Mandatum∴ccord. The rule that made secrets into debts and debts into erasure. Noctisum~ announced it with the practiced cadence of a merchant closing a deal he'd already won, and the aurora-light flickered as if the Berg held its breath, and thirty-seven penguins in that chamber understood that the Mist~Record on the table was not a relic but a lever. The Order had read it. The Order knew what the deep ice remembered. And now, under the Accord, every reader's fate would be documented—witnessed testimony, blood-debt, accumulating like interest on a loan no one had agreed to take.
    
    The cracked fire-core amulet at Noctisum~'s throat caught the light. The crack was too clean for accident; other merchants noticed but did not ask. There were proverbs for that. *What the fire remembers, the ice forgets.* What Noctisum~ had seen during his own reading of the Mist~Record—the session that left him mid-sentence, the sentence he never finished—was itself an inheritance. He passed it to every penguin who noticed the crack and chose not to speak.
    
    The tome sat on the obsidian ice, warm as a living thing. The Order's archivists polished it every third day. Three seasons later, the E'frostlings reclaimed Terrace Momiou, and the Vum∴tenebra ate everything the Order had built, and they called it *necessary clarity* until the tunnel-systems froze mid-collapse and the smell of char and brine and something older filled the galleries for a generation.
    
    A second notch was carved into the spine. The records name the reader. No one repeats it.
    
    ---
    
    ☽'laestiala unwrapped the cloth and did not touch what was underneath.
    
    The vault beneath Scar☽ Lucamlinaecius was cold in the way that old wounds are cold—not from temperature but from absence. The fire-cores in the wall-brackets had been changed on schedule, and the bioluminescent cloth that swaddled the Mist~Record was the same cloth the Order's archivists had used, and it had never been changed, because changing it would mean touching the book, and no one touched the book.
    
    Two notches in the spine. A frost-crack running through the back cover where someone had tried to burn the tome during the Clever Ice Age—the records didn't say who, and the remnants of the Covens' Order wouldn't clarify, and under Mandatum∴ccord that silence was technically a debt, but no one was collecting debts from ghosts. The volcanic glass had chipped along one edge. The bone binding had yellowed, then darkened, like a bruise aging through its colors.
    
    The tome was still warm.
    
    ☽'laestiala was the keeper by default. Her mother had been keeper by refusal—she'd guarded the vault for eleven seasons and never once opened the cloth, passing that refusal forward as a kind of instruction more effective than any reading. Her mother's mother had been the last to request a reading, the scholar Tuk'krek, who'd folded the cloth exactly as it still lay and walked out and spoken to no one about what she'd found. Seven seasons of silence; then death by cold-burn, complications secondary to deep-vein exposure, duration unrecorded.
    
    The Mandatum∴ccord ledger for the Mist~Record was now thicker than the tome itself. Every reader catalogued. Every fate witnessed, testified, entered into the colony's recitation cycle. The fire-tenders on the deep shifts below sang fragments of the Scar-Mouth Song—the work-chant that encoded warnings no one had assembled into a complete verse since ☽'voidae vanished. The refrain was unlucky to sing above mid-depth. Below mid-depth, it was simply true.
    
    ☽'laestiala wrapped the cloth back over the warm glass. She did not read. She did not speak. She reported to the shadow-parliament that the artifact was intact, which was a word that meant *still here, still warm, still waiting*, and the shadow-parliament noted her report and did not ask questions, because questions were debts, and debts corrupted the ice's memory, and the ice remembered enough.
    
    She held the book; the book held longer.
    
    The third notch was carved by someone who left no name. The records say only: *reader entered the vault during the Long Dark. Reader did not emerge. The cloth was found refolded.*
    
    ---
    
    Totatirus~ Domina does not read the Mist~Record. She does not unwrap it. She does not open the vault on any schedule. She sits in her stone shelter on the western ledge with her three journals and her ciphers and her braziers she won't let die, and the vent below carries steam that smells of deep-earth and old fire, and she burns one page at a time into that steam, as if the Berg's lungs might carry what she cannot say aloud.
    
    The tome is beneath her. Thirty meters of ice and stone, in the shadow-forge vault, in the bioluminescent cloth that has never been changed. Three notches in the spine. The volcanic glass chipped, the bone binding dark with age, the fire-core warmth persisting beyond reason—beyond the exhaustion cycle of any crystal ever mined, beyond the shelf-life of any forge-work the Nightshelf has produced. The warmth is its own argument. The warmth is what makes her afraid.
    
    Her sister Kael leaves supplies at the ledge's edge. Dried krill. A fire-core, fresh from the mid-depth veins. A silence that carries more weight than the proverbs of earlier eras, because Kael's entire bloodline died at Terrace Momiou, and she still leaves fire-core offerings at the frozen edge, and she does not explain, and no one asks.
    
    Totatirus~ turns away delegations. Three so far. She warned off the orca scouts who breached the ice nearby—whether this makes her traitor or guardian, the colony cannot decide, and she has stopped caring which.
    
    The pages only speak in darkness. She knows this. Every keeper knew this. The difference is that Totatirus~ no longer distinguishes between the darkness inside the vault and the darkness inside the keeper. She has become the quarantine. Warm, intact, and utterly alone.
    
    Somewhere below, the tome waits in cloth that smells of a generation that believed knowledge could be claimed without consequence. Its fire-core heart beats slow and patient. The ice remembers. The watchers at the edge of perception press close during the Long Dark, and Totatirus~ sits above them and burns her pages into the steam, and the story does not end.
    
    It just leaves her view.
  CANONICAL_CHRONICLE
  annotation "note_1771576261901_0", anchor: "Of everything under the ice. Everything that was here before us.", text: "I have seen those impressions. In the deepest strata of the Foundation Depths, below the blue ice, there are shapes the archive has no taxonomy for. Not penguin, not orca, not anything the geological record accounts for. Silvsiaelrous read a record of them and wept. I have catalogued their frozen shadows alone for years and cannot say she was wrong to.", type: :tangent, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771576261901_1", anchor: "The ice had taken her during the Abyss Collapse Tide or she'd walked into it", text: "The distinction matters. 'Walked into it' is the Walking — the compulsion that seizes the grieving, the ones who simply leave and do not return. 'Taken' is geological event, structural failure, a death with a cause. That this generation stopped distinguishing between the two tells you more about what the Abyss Collapse Tide did to the colony's capacity for grief than any casualty figure could.", type: :commentary, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771576261901_2", anchor: "A frost-crack running through the back cover where someone had tried to burn the tome during the Clever Ice Age", text: "The Clever Ice Age postdates this chronicle's stated scope by a full era. The damage pattern is real — I have seen immolation protocols consistent with Clever Ice Age artifact-purging in other Nightshelf collections. But the chronicler cannot possess this information within the temporal frame they established. Most likely source contamination: a later keeper's oral account absorbed into ☽'laestiala's section without attribution. The ice layers things. Chroniclers sometimes forget they are not ice.", type: :temporal, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771576261901_3", anchor: "She warned off the orca scouts who breached the ice nearby", text: "'Warned off.' Orca pods are coordinated military intelligences conducting blood-rituals to wake a dead god. They do not get warned off by a lone hermit on a ledge. Either Totatirus~ negotiated — which implies contact the colony would rather not acknowledge — or this is Incursion-era framing projected backward onto Faction Wars raiding patterns. The language of directed reconnaissance belongs to war-pods, not opportunistic scouts.", type: :skepticism, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771576261901_4", anchor: "reader entered the vault during the Long Dark. Reader did not emerge. The cloth was found refolded.", text: "During the Long Dark the veil thins. The dead speak more clearly. Presences press close. Someone entered absolute darkness with a tome that speaks only in darkness, during the season when the boundary between memory and presence dissolves. The cloth was refolded. By whom.", type: :commentary, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771576261901_5", anchor: "She did not read. She did not speak.", text: "The Nightshelf's finest record-keeping has always been the things they chose not to do. I mean this without irony.", type: :commentary, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771576261901_6", anchor: "knowledge could be claimed without consequence", text: "The Great Thaw's epitaph. Every tunnel they cut, every vein they opened, every tome they carried into the light. The frontier remembered differently.", type: :commentary, display: :popout
